AB  - Using questionnaire survey data from 296 farmers in Dezhou, Shandong, this paper finds that most farmers recognize the phenomenon of climate change and its impacts on agricultural production. Gender, educational level, household per capita income and livestock income significantly affect farmers' perceptions of climate change. Fewer than half of respondents reported having adopted climate change adaptation behaviours. Farmers' level of perception of climate change and gender significantly affect whether they adopt adaptive behaviours. The adaptive behaviours adopted by farmers are mainly passive ones such as adjusting farming timing, increasing inputs and irrigation, supplemented by proactive ones such as changing crop varieties, building infrastructure, adopting new technologies and improving the ecological environment around farmland. Farmers' level of perception of climate change, age and educational level significantly affect whether they adopt proactive adaptive behaviours.
