AB  - That vulnerability in responding to climate change leads to poverty traps has been confirmed by many case studies, but this is only one aspect of how climate change affects the distribution of wealth. From a more comprehensive perspective, the process of climate change adaptation redefines "winners" and "losers" and the gap between them, and understanding this overall process is key to whether poverty reduction can succeed. Taking a gacha (village) on the Hulunbuir grassland of Inner Mongolia as a case, and based on meteorological and fieldwork data, this paper finds that poverty traps and elite capture act simultaneously, making local wealth polarization increasingly pronounced: some herding households obtain more project support on the basis of their economic and social capital and their disaster resistance capacity increases, while other herding households, owing to poor infrastructure and lack of funds, have fewer and fewer livestock and ultimately become households without livestock. Comparing these two types of herding households shows that relying solely on project subsidies can hardly achieve poverty reduction goals; only by improving herding households' disaster response capacity can they be prevented from falling into poverty traps and the gap between rich and poor in pastoral areas be narrowed.
