AB  - The climate change pose multiple pressure on welfare and livelihoods of agricultural population, especially the poorest ones based on natural resources living in nature reserves, hence it is crucial for the government to understand the perception and adaption mechanism of farmers on climate change and enact efficient policies. This study used the 234 rural household data surveyed randomly in 4 nature reserves in Guangdong Province, analyzed the factors of farmers' perception and adaption behaviors on climate change, the result showed that: education level, farmland area, and owning computers or not affected farmers perception on climate change. However, the factors affecting farmers' perception and adaption behaviors existed some difference, farmers participated the training course of nature reserve, with higher education level, more farmland area, and living closer to the market were more likely to adopt adaption measures, while farmers with higher income diversification index, higher annual income per capita were less likely to adopt adaption measures.
