TI  - The Theory and Practice of Adaptation to Climate Change in Ecologically-Vulnerable Communities in the Western China
TT  - 西部生态脆弱社区气候变化适应性的理论与实践探索
AU  - 庞淼
KW  - climate change adaptability
KW  - ecological fragility
KW  - poverty
AB  - Climate change is a global environmental problem with marked complexity, long-term character and externality. The trend of climate change characterized prominently by global warming, and the mainstream IPCC judgement that human activities have caused warming, are the premise and basis of this study. Climate change poses a particularly great threat to ecologically fragile rural communities in western China. In such regions of high ecological sensitivity and vulnerability, relative poverty and multicultural integration, human activities cause more serious damage to the resources and environment on which people depend for survival, and the contradiction between farmers' extreme poverty and the urgent need for environmental protection is most acute. This paper reviews the theoretical origins and development of climate change adaptation, analyses the impacts of climate change on ecologically fragile rural communities, examines community-level climate change adaptation actions, summarizes community lessons and reflections on climate change adaptation in ecologically fragile communities in western China, and puts forward suggestions for exploring models of climate change adaptation in these communities.
