AU  - 王雪纯
AU  - 杨秀
KW  - climate change adaptation
KW  - adaptation collaboration
KW  - collaborative governance
KW  - collaborative advantage theory
AB  - The climate change problem has global negative externalities and iterative systemic impacts, and therefore particularly requires collaborative governance. This paper analyses the theoretical basis of collaborative governance in climate change adaptation action and selects two relatively representative collaborative mechanisms in the international community for case studies: Italy's bottom-up social networks leading national adaptation action, and the US top-down, presidentially launched international public-private partnership. The study finds that when the national level has not yet recognized the importance of climate change adaptation, the non-governmental societal level appears in public view as a forerunner; in the early stage of adaptation work, knowledge sharing and learning can effectively link a series of social networks; and leadership with legitimate and administrative authority in collaborative governance attracts many founding organizations and investors to join. China can draw on international experience in collaborative adaptation governance by focusing on information transfer and knowledge system building and broadly including important societal participants such as research institutions and social organizations; by relying on the existing climate-resilient city pilots to enhance the collaborative leadership of local governments and implement technology introduction and financial investment; and by working with partner countries through the Belt and Road Initiative to jointly address climate change.
