AB  - As one of the most severe challenges facing human society, climate change raises issues of intergenerational justice. Climate change will infringe upon the interests and rights of future generations. In responding to climate change, the present generation bears retrospective intergenerational obligations (focusing on corrective justice) and prospective intergenerational obligations (focusing on distributive justice); the former mainly require "adaptation" to climate change, and the latter mainly require "mitigation" of climate change. To distribute the costs of adapting to and mitigating climate change fairly, a composite solution combining a "moderate polluter-pays principle" and the "ability-to-pay principle" can be adopted to respond to climate change.
