AB  - Based on a social-ecological system(SES)framework, this paper explores the complex dynamic relationships between agricultural resource systems and governance systems under climate change scenarios, revealing the theoretical relationship of how climate change affects the supply, access, utilization, and stability of food security in China. It also applies Meta-regression to explain the reasons for the differences in quantitative findings on food supply security, and analyzes the strategies for mitigating and adapting to the impacts of climate change in terms of the various aspects of food production, supply, consumption, and import and export.This paper finds that the SES framework helps to analyze the complex dynamic linkages in the coupled adaptation process of climate change and food security.The projections of food production changes have produced differentiated results depending on the settings of climate scenarios, regions, crop types, climate characteristics, and adaptation measures.The SES framework provides a common language for integrating multidisciplinary research findings.Overall, this paper combines social and ecosystem perspectives through the SES framework, providing a novel perspective for a more comprehensive understanding of the complex dynamic linkages between climate change and food security.
