KW  - climate change
KW  - adaptation policy
KW  - human rights-based approach
AB  - In response to the threats to human rights posed by climate change, states have generally adopted national climate change adaptation policies to reduce climate vulnerability and enhance climate resilience. However, national climate change adaptation policies themselves are prone to predictable human rights harm due to issues such as inadequate infrastructure planning and uneven resource allocation. For this reason, states should follow basic human rights principles when formulating climate change adaptation policies. However, empirical research shows that most national climate change adaptation policies still lack a rights-based perspective, mainly due to the structural neglect of human rights in national priorities, which in turn leads to insu fficient participation of stakeholders, especially climate-vulnerable groups, in the process of formulating national climate change adaptation policies. Governments should therefore adopt a human rights-based approach to the formulation and implementation of national climate change adaptation policies, promoting climate empowerment by raising public awareness of climate adaptation, facilitating meaningful stakeholder participation in the process, focusing on climate-vulnerable groups in terms of content, realizing climate justice, and enhancing transparency and the effectiveness of implementation by improving policy coherence and putting in place monitoring and accountability mechanisms.
