AB  - Against the background of global climate change posing a significant threat to human society and the natural environment, exploring climate justice and identifying and supporting climate-vulnerable populations has become an urgent task for all countries. From the perspective of climate justice, this paper examines the impacts of climate change on vulnerable communities and analyses in depth the effectiveness and shortcomings of current protective measures. Through a literature review and case analysis, it defines the concept of climate-vulnerable communities and analyses the complex and multi-level challenges they face. From national policies to local measures, covering legislation, financial support and technical assistance, existing policies and practices remain deficient in identifying vulnerable communities, assessing climate impacts and implementing specific protective measures. Based on these issues, this paper proposes a series of improvements, including refining identification mechanisms for climate-vulnerable communities, enhancing the targeting and flexibility of policies, promoting multi-sectoral cooperation, and increasing support and investment for community-level adaptation actions, with a view to providing theoretical support for achieving climate justice in the climate change adaptation process.
