AB  - In the face of intensifying climate change, food security is under growing pressure. This study develops a theoretical framework for climate response and adaptation across the full food supply chain, covering production, storage, transportation, and trade. It identifies key pathways through which climate change affects food security and highlights blind spots in the current policy system. Findings show that climate change impacts food production via declining yields, cropping adjustments, and changes in total factor productivity, while also destabilizing supply chains and global trade. Current policies show gaps in production-supply coordination, technology adoption incentives, and cost-sharing mechanisms between producing and consuming regions. The paper proposes three strategies: building a coordinated adaptation system across the supply chain, enhancing incentives for technology adoption, and developing a horizontal compensation mechanism that includes climate-related costs. These measures can strengthen the resilience of the food system and improve the fairness and sustainability of food security governance.
