AB  - Owing to insufficient climate adaptation capacity and weak infrastructure, Africa is one of the continents most severely affected by global climate change. The State of the Climate in Africa 2024 report released by the World Meteorological Organization in May 2025 pointed out that in 2024 the African continent suffered repeated waves of extreme weather such as droughts, floods and heatwaves, severely impacting agriculture, public health, infrastructure, education and other sectors, and further exacerbating Africa's food security crisis and social vulnerability. Facing increasingly severe climate challenges, although African countries are actively advancing climate adaptation policies, developing renewable energy and improving disaster early warning mechanisms, they still face multiple difficulties in finance, technology and governance capacity. In this context, China and African countries have carried out active and in-depth cooperation in clean energy, climate finance, climate change adaptation capacity building and other fields, making China an important external force driving Africa's green transition and sustainable development.
