TI  - Epistemic Injustice in the Technological Innovation of Climate-Smart Agriculture
TT  - 气候智慧型农业创新发展中的认知不公及其应对
AU  - 滕菲
KW  - climate-smart agriculture
KW  - epistemic injustice
KW  - indigenous knowledge
KW  - epistemological pluralism
AB  - In response to climate change, the United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization(FAO) introduced climate-smart agriculture(CSA), a framework designed to integrate climate change mitigation, adaptation, and food security objectives. Current implementation focuses on advancing sustainable agricultural transformation in developing countries and particularly vulnerable regions, primarily via technological innovation. Artificial intelligence offers considerable promise for advancing CSA, yet simultaneously raises a range of ethical issues.This study investigates the underlying causes of ethical issues associated with climate-smart agriculture. It demonstrates that epistemic injustice, triggered by the agricultural intelligence revolution, constitutes a critical factor in generating ethical issues.Furthermore, the research analyzes the structural mechanisms underpinning epistemic injustice and formulates methods to address these systemic inequities.The examine of the issue of epistemic injustice is from dual analytical dimensions social critique and technological critique.Fricker's theory of epistemic injustice represents a significant departure from traditional social justice frameworks, which predominantly focus on disparities in resource allocation, opportunity distribution, or power dynamics. By incorporating the deprivation of epistemic agency into critical analysis, Fricker's framework provides a novel theoretical lens for social critique.However, social critiques grounded in epistemic injustice did not fully integrate an epistemological critique of technology itself.To address this gap, this study introduces a pluralistic epistemological perspective to conduct a critical examination of artificial intelligence.(1) Climate-smart agriculture causes epistemic injustice toward traditional agricultural communities by exacerbating power asymmetries between agribusiness corporations and local farmers, as well as marginalizing indigenous knowledge in technological practices.(2)From the perspective of social critique,the epistemic injustice inherent in CSA exacerbate power asymmetries among social groups and generate ethical dilemmas. As a structural bias, its remediation requires integrated approaches: fostering equitable dialogue among stakeholders(including technical experts, local policymakers, and agrarian communities); cultivating individual epistemic virtues; and restructuring societal inequities. While this approach expands the traditional paradigm of social justice—predominantly focused on resource/opportunity/power distribution—it neglects the constitutive role of technological artifacts in shaping epistemic injustice.(3)In the digital transformation of agriculture, Artificial Intelligence(AI) has emerged as a pivotal force. However, its adherence to a computational framework reductively equates human cognition with data processing, thereby neglecting the critical role of contextual dependency in cognitive activities. This constitutes an epistemological limitation inherent to AI's technical paradigm.(4)Artificial intelligence is not a value-neutral technical instrument but a technological artifact embedded with specific epistemic presuppositions. However, current technological paradigms regard it as a universally and neutral tool. This conceptual mistake constitutes the epistemic injustice in smart agriculture.This study advocates the necessitates of integrating pluralistic epistemologies into technological practices. Establishing a symbiotic relationship between technology and socio-cultural contexts is a viable method. Strengthening contextual awareness enhances the adaptability and inclusiveness of technical systems. Situated technological design bridges the tension between technical rationality and humanistic values, offering a feasible pathway toward the construction of inclusive technological innovation systems.
