AB  - In recent years, enhancing agricultural climate resilience has become a central issue in addressing climate change impacts, ensuring food security, and advancing rural revitalization. This paper systematically reviews Chinese and international research on agricultural climate resilience from 2007 to 2026, analyzes the evolution of its conceptual connotation from a multidisciplinary perspective, and constructs a three-dimensional interactive framework comprising subject, mediator, and object. In this framework, farm households serve as the core subject, digital technologies, policy and institutional arrangements, and market mechanisms function as linking mediators, and agricultural production systems constitute the object. The results show that agricultural climate resilience has evolved from a single-system attribute into a comprehensive concept encompassing resistance, adaptability, and reconfiguration capacity. Its enhancement does not depend on the unidirectional effect of any single factor, but rather on the synergistic coupling among actor responses, production system adaptation, and external support mechanisms. Existing research can be further advanced in conceptual integration, mechanism analysis, and methodological innovation. Future studies should further examine the interactive mechanisms among actor decision-making, production system evolution, and technology-institution-market environments within an interdisciplinary theoretical framework. Methodologically, greater integration of multi-agent simulation, machine learning, and participatory modeling can help reveal how administrative structures, digital governance, and social equity shape agricultural climate resilience. Such efforts will support the incorporation of climate resilience governance into spatial planning and national climate adaptation strategies, thereby providing theoretical insights and policy references for building a climate-resilient agricultural system.
