AB  - Climate change poses systemic challenges to food security across production, circulation, reserves, consumption and nutritional security. Based on a whole-chain perspective, this paper analyses the multidimensional mechanisms through which climate change affects food security. It reveals China's current prominent shortcomings in translating climate early warnings into action, technology extension, cultivated land degradation, biodiversity conservation, cross-border risk prevention, and synergy with emission reduction. The study proposes improving whole-chain climate adaptation mechanisms, removing bottlenecks in early warning, loss reduction and technology extension, and improving the benefit compensation system between producing and consuming regions; accelerating surveys and dynamic zoning of agricultural climate resources, managing cultivated land degradation by category, and strengthening biodiversity conservation and cross-border risk early warning; implementing adaptation assistance for smallholder farmers, and coordinating the synergistic development of carbon emission reduction and food security. Deeply embedding climate resilience into every link of the food system is the key pathway to shifting from passive response to proactive adaptation and consolidating the sustainable foundation of national food security.
