AB  - The functional positioning of catastrophe insurance has gradually expanded from post-disaster economic compensation to a comprehensive risk management mechanism giving equal weight to pre-disaster risk prevention, risk-spreading arrangements during disasters, and coordinated post-disaster recovery and governance. The Outline of the 15th Five-Year Plan explicitly proposes to "improve the climate change adaptation work system, strengthen assessment of the regional risk impacts of climate change on key vulnerable sectors, and enhance capacity to respond to climate change, especially extreme weather". In the context of climate change, the frequency, loss intensity and systemic impacts of natural disaster risks continue to rise, posing severe challenges to the safety of people's lives and property and to economic and social stability. As an important institutional tool linking government governance, market mechanisms and social security, catastrophe insurance is widely regarded worldwide as a key lever for addressing low-frequency, high-loss disaster risks, and its functional positioning has gradually expanded from post-disaster economic compensation to a comprehensive risk management mechanism giving equal weight to pre-disaster risk prevention, risk-spreading arrangements during disasters, and coordinated post-disaster recovery and governance.
