AB  - Climate change has gradually evolved into a climate crisis in the 21 st century, profoundly reshaping the relationship between humans and nature. While early anthropological attention to climate was largely implicit in critiques of climatic/environmental determinism, it was not until the deepening global climate crisis that climate change gradually emerged as a core anthropological concern, giving rise to climate anthropology as a new disciplinary branch. From the perspective of climate anthropology, this paper analyzes how different groups develop new livelihood strategies through technological adaptations while maintaining their connection with nature through cultural adaptations in response to climate change. However, the interplay of power dynamics and socioeconomic factors in the process of capitalist globalization has led to frequent occurrences of "pseudo-adaptation" phenomena. Prioritizing capital accumulation over ecological justice and social equity, these adaptation approaches exacerbate inequalities in humanto-human and human-nature relationships through embedded power structures. Confronting such "pseudo-adaptation" practices, mere technological adjustments or exclusive reliance on traditional knowledge prove insufficient. We must integrate cultural, scientific, social, and political dimensions to reconceptualize human-nature relationships in climate change contexts, thereby exploring harmonious climate adaptation approaches that harmony between humanity and nature.
