AB  - Against the backdrop of the Paris Agreement formally establishing the role of traditional knowledge in climate change adaptation, global climate governance is witnessing a new trend of knowledge integration that moves beyond a singular scientific paradigm. This paper aims to explore the construction of discourse power for traditional knowledge within this new paradigm and China's corresponding participation strategies. It systematically traces the evolution of the traditional knowledge agenda within the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change(UNFCCC) framework, from initial "recognition" and "attention" to its gradual "incorporation" into the governance system, and analyzes the institutional channels for participation, such as the Local Communities and Indigenous Peoples Platform established by the Paris Agreement. The study finds three predominant discursive tendencies regarding traditional knowledge in the international community: the "scientific information supplement" view represented by Western countries, the "rights and culture" view held by indigenous communities, and the "inclusive ecological knowledge" view advocated by major developing countries like China and India. Facing this landscape, China's participation in global climate governance through traditional knowledge encounters challenges such as reliance on Western paradigms in the international discourse system and insufficient involvement of domestic philosophy and social sciences, but also possesses advantages like pioneering domestic biodiversity conservation policies and a rich repository of traditional ecological wisdom. Therefore, this paper proposes that China should actively construct and enhance its discourse power in the field of traditional knowledge through three main pathways: deepening participation in international agenda discussions, promoting the organic integration of traditional knowledge with national climate policies, and proactively sharing Chinese practices and wisdom. These efforts aim to contribute Chinese strength to shaping a new, fair, and cooperative paradigm for global climate governance.
